"[2602.00594v1] Kanade: A Simple Disentangled Tokenizer for Spoken Language Modeling (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.00594v1)\nciteturn31495view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31494view4\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 31 Jan 2026 (this version), latest version 5 May 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Kanade: A Simple Disentangled Tokenizer for Spoken Language Modeling\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Zhijie Huang , cite7†Stephen McIntosh , cite8†Daisuke Saito , cite9†Nobuaki Minematsu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Kanade: A Simple Disentangled Tokenizer for Spoken Language Modeling, by Zhijie Huang and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:A good language model starts with a good tokenizer. Tokenization is especially important for speech modeling, which must handle continuous signals that mix linguistic and non-linguistic information. A speech tokenizer should extract phonetics and prosody, suppress linguistically irrelevant information like speaker identity, and enable high-quality synthesis. We present Kanade, a single-layer disentangled speech tokenizer that realizes this ideal.\nL17: Kanade separates out acoustic constants to create a single stream of tokens that captures rich phonetics and prosody. It does so without the need for auxiliary methods that existing disentangled codecs often rely on. Experiments show that Kanade achieves state-of-the-art speaker disentanglement and lexical availability, while maintaining excellent reconstruction quality.\nL18: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Sound (cs.SD); Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)\nL19: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2602.00594 [cs.CL]\nL20:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2602.00594v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL21:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.00594†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL22: ## Submission history\nL23: \nL24: From: Zhijie Huang [cite15†view email ]\nL25: [v1] Sat, 31 Jan 2026 08:20:39 UTC (2,483 KB)\nL26: cite5†[v2] Tue, 5 May 2026 13:18:46 UTC (2,492 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Kanade: A Simple Disentangled Tokenizer for Spoken Language Modeling, by Zhijie Huang and 3 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite10†View PDF L35:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite16†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CL\nL42: \nL43: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L44: \nL45: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2026-02 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite23†cs L50: cite24†cs.SD L51: cite25†eess L52: cite26†eess.AS L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite27†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite29†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite30†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite31†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL84: \nL85: Litmaps (cite32†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co