arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL154: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite45†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL155: \nL156: cite46†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite47†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL157: \nL158: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite48†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL159: cite49†About†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite54†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite55†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite56†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L160: \nL161: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGLM-OCR - Overview - Z.AI DEVELOPER DOCUMENT (https://docs.z.ai/guides/vlm/glm-ocr)\nciteturn31495view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31494view5\",\"lineno\":47}); Total lines: 222\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\nL1: >\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/llms.txt L3: >\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \nL8: cite2†Overview - Z.AI DEVELOPER DOCUMENT home page†z.ai L9: \nL10: Search...\nL11: \nL12: Navigation\nL13: \nL14: Models\nL15: \nL16: GLM-OCR\nL17: \nL18: cite3†Guides cite4†API Reference cite5†Coding Plan cite6†Released Notes cite7†Terms and Policy cite8†Help Center L19: \nL20: Models\nL21: \nL22: # GLM-OCR\nL23: \nL24: Copy page Copy page\nL25: ##\nL26: \nL27: cite9†​ L28: \nL29: Overview\nL30: \nL31: GLM-OCR is a lightweight professional OCR model with parameters as small as 0.9B, yet it achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple capabilities. It sets a new benchmark for document parsing with its “small size and high accuracy.” Key features include:\nL32:   * Performance SOTA: Scored 94.62 points to top OmniDocBench V1.5 and achieved current best performance across multiple mainstream document understanding benchmarks including tables and formulas at launch.\nL33:   * Optimized for Real-World Scenarios: Delivers stable, leading accuracy in complex environments like code documentation, intricate tables, and stamp recognition. Maintains exceptional recognition precision even with complex layouts, diverse fonts, or mixed text-image content.\nL34:   * Efficient and Cost-Effective: With just 0.9B parameters, supports VLLM and SGLang deployment, significantly reducing inference latency and computational overhead.\nL35: ## Input Modality\nL36: \nL37:   * PDF, images (JPG, PNG)\nL38:   * Single image ≤ 10MB, PDF ≤ 50MB\nL39:   * Maximum support: 100 pages\nL40: \nL41: ## Output Modality\nL42: \nL43: Text / Image Links / MD Documents\nL44: \nL45: ## Supported Language\nL46: \nL47: Support Chinese, English, French, Spanish, Russian, German, Japanese, Korean, etc.\nL48: \nL49: For detailed pricing information on GLM-OCR, please visit the cite10†Pricing Page .\nL50: ##\nL51: \nL52: cite11†​ L53: \nL54: Usage\nL55: \nL56: Text Recognition\nL57: \nL58: Recognize text content from photos, screenshots, documents, and scans, supporting printed text, handwriting, and mathematical formulas. Applicable to diverse scenarios including education, research, and office work.\nL59: \nL60: Table Recognition\nL61: \nL62: Identify table structures and content, converting them into HTML-formatted sequences. Suitable for scenarios involving table data entry, conversion, and editing.\nL63: \nL64: Information Structuring\nL65: Extract key information from various cards, certificates, receipts, and forms, outputting structured JSON data. Supports applications in banking, insurance, government services, legal, logistics, and other industries.\nL66: \nL67: Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)\nL68: \nL69: Support high-volume document recognition and parsing with high accuracy and standardized output formats, providing a robust foundation for RAG.\nL70: ##\nL71: \nL72: cite12†​ L73: \nL74: Resources\nL75: \nL76:   * cite13†API Documentation : Learn how to call the API.\nL77: \nL78: ##\nL79: \nL80: cite14†​ L81: \nL82: Introducing GLM-OCR\nL83: \nL84: 1\nL85: ### State-of-the-Art Performance, Precision in Action\nL86: Thanks to its proprietary CogViT visual encoder and deep scene optimization, GLM-OCR achieves “compact size, high accuracy.”With only 0.9B parameters, GLM-OCR achieved SOTA on the authoritative document parsing benchmark OmniDocBench V1.5 with a score of 94.6.\nL87: It outperforms multiple specialized OCR models across four key domains—text, formula, table recognition, and information extraction—with performance approaching that of Gemini-3-Pro.cite15†Image: Description†cdn.bigmodel.cn Beyond public benchmarks, we conducted internal evaluations across six core real-world scenarios.\nL88: Results show GLM-OCR delivers significant advantages across dimensions including code documentation, real-world tables, handwriting, multilingual text, seal recognition, and invoice extraction.cite16†Image: Description†cdn.bigmodel.cn L89: 2\nL90: ### Faster, More Cost-Effective\nL91: For speed, we compared different OCR methods under identical hardware and testing conditions (single replica, single concurrency), evaluating their performance in parsing and exporting Markdown files from both image and PDF inputs.\nL92: Results show GLM-OCR achieves a throughput of 1.86 pages/second for PDF documents and 0.67 images/second for images, significantly outperforming comparable models.cite17†Image: Description†cdn.bigmodel.cn Pricing is uniform for both API input and output, costing just $0.03 per million tokens.\nL93: ##\nL94: \nL95: cite18†​ L96: \nL97: Examples\nL98: \nL99:   * Code Block Recognition\nL100: \nL101:   * Complex Chart Content Recognition\nL102: \nL103:   * Bill Recognition\nL104: \nL105:   * Handwriting Recognition\nL106: \nL107: ## Input\nL108: \nL109: cite19†Image: Description†cdn.bigmodel.cn L110: \nL111: ## Output\nL112: \nL113: cite20†Image: Description†cdn.bigmodel.cn L114: ##\nL115: \nL116: cite21†​ L117: \nL118: Quick Start\nL119: \nL120:   * cURL\nL121: \nL122:   * Python\nL123: \nL124:   * Java\nL125: \nL126:     `curl --location --request POST 'https://api.z.ai/api/paas/v4/layout_parsing' \\\nL127:     --header 'Authorization: Bearer your-api-key' \\\nL128:     --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\nL129:     --data-raw '{\nL130:       \"model\": \"glm-ocr\",\nL131:       \"file\": \"https://cdn.bigmodel.cn/static/logo/introduction.png\"\nL132:     }'\nL133:     `\nL134: Install SDK\nL135: \nL136:     `# Install the latest version\nL137:     pip install zai-sdk\nL138:     # Or specify a version\nL139:     pip install zai-sdk==0.2.3\nL140:     `\nL141: \nL142: Verify installation\nL143: \nL144:     `import zai\nL145:     print(zai.__version__)\nL146:     `\nL147: Basic Call\nL148: \nL149:     `from zai import ZaiClient\nL150: \nL151:     # Initialize client\nL152:     client = ZaiClient(api_key=\"your-api-key\")\nL153: \nL154:     image_url = \"https://cdn.bigmodel.cn/static/logo/introduction.png\"\nL155: \nL156:     # Call layout parsing API\nL157:     response = client.layout_parsing.create(\nL158:         model=\"glm-ocr\",\nL159:         file=image_url\nL160:     )\nL161: \nL162:     # Output result\nL163:     print(response)\nL164:     `\nL165: Install SDK Maven\nL166: \nL167:     `<dependency>\nL168:       <groupId>ai.z.openapi</groupId>\nL169:       <artifactId>zai-sdk</artifactId>\nL170:       <version>0.3.5</version>\nL171:     </dependency>\nL172:     `\nL173: \nL174: Gradle (Groovy)\nL175: \nL176:     `implementation 'ai.z.openapi:zai-sdk:0.3.5'\nL177:     `\nL178: Basic Call\nL179: \nL180:     `import ai.z.openapi.ZaiClient;\nL181:     import ai.z.openapi.service.layoutparsing.LayoutParsingCreateParams;\nL182:     import ai.z.openapi.service.layoutparsing.LayoutParsingResponse;\nL183:     import ai.z.openapi.service.layoutparsing.LayoutParsingResult;\nL184: \nL185:     public class LayoutParsing {\nL186:         public static void main(String[] args) {\nL187:             // Initialize client\nL188:             ZaiClient client = ZaiClient.builder()\nL189:                 .ofZAI()\nL190:                 .apiKey(\"your-api-key\")\nL191:                 .build();\nL192: \nL193:             String model = \"glm-ocr\";\nL194:             String file = \"https://cdn.bigmodel.cn/static/logo/introduction.png\";\nL195: \nL196:             // Create layout parsing request\nL197:             LayoutParsingCreateParams params = LayoutParsingCreateParams.builder()\nL198:                 .model(model)\nL199:                 .file(file)\nL200:                 .build();\nL201: \nL202:             // Send request\nL203:             LayoutParsingResponse response = client.layoutParsing().layoutParsing(params);\nL204:             // Handle response\nL205:             if (response.isSuccess()) {\nL206:                 System.out.println(\"Parsing result: \" + response.getData());\nL207:             } else {\nL208:                 System.err.println(\"Error: \" + response.getMsg());\nL209:             }\nL210:         }\nL211:     }\nL212:     `\nL213: Was this page helpful?\nL214: \nL215: Yes No\nL216: \nL217: cite22†GLM-5.2 cite23†GLM-ASR-2512 L218: \nL219: cite24†x†x.com cite25†github†github.com cite26†discord†discord.gg cite27†linkedin†www.linkedin.com L220: \nL221: cite28†Powered byThis documentation is built and hosted on Mintlify, a developer documentation platform†www.mintlify.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1Introduction (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.03560v1)\nciteturn31495view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.03560v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 361\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Background & Motivation L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Training-free vs. Trainable Sparse Attention L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Hybrid Attention Architecture L20:     3. cite10†2.3 Cross-Layer Salient Token Stability L21:     4. cite11†2.4 Cross-layer KV Cache Sharing L22:   4. cite12†3 Methodology L23:     1. cite13†3.1 HySparse Overview L24:     2. cite14†3.2 Full Attention Layers L25:     3. cite15†3.3 Sparse Attention Layers L26:   5. cite16†4 Experiments L27:     1. cite17†4.1 Experiments Setup L28:       1. cite18†Model Configuration L29:       2. cite19†Training Hyper-parameters L30:       3. cite20†Evaluation Benchmark L31:     2. cite21†4.2 Performance of HySparse on General Benchmarks L32:       1. cite22†7B Dense Performance L33:       2. cite23†Scaling to 80B MoE L34:     3. cite24†4.3 Long-context Benchmarks L35:     4. cite25†4.4 Ablation Study L36:       1. cite26†Study 1: Intra-layer Hybridization with SWA L37:       2. cite27†Study 2: Cross-layer KV Cache Sharing Configuration L38:   6. cite28†5 Discussion & Future Works L39:     1. cite29†Can We Ultimately Avoid Full Attention? L40:     2. cite30†Potential of HySparse for Efficient KV Cache Offloading L41:   7. cite31†6 Conclusion L42:   8. cite32†References L43: cite33†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L44: \nL45: arXiv:2602.03560v1 [cs.CL] 03 Feb 2026\nL46: \nL47: \\titlefont\nL48: \nL49: HySparse: A Hybrid Sparse Attention Architecture\nL50: \nL51: with Oracle Token Selection and KV Cache Sharing\nL52: \nL53: Yizhao Gao Jianyu Wei Qihao Zhang Yu Cheng Shimao Chen\nL54: Zhengju Tang Zihan Jiang Yifan Song Hailin Zhang Liang Zhao\nL55: Bo Yang Gang Wang Shijie Cao Fuli Luo^{⋄}\nL56: \nL57: LLM-Core Xiaomi\nL58: \nL59: \\abscontent\nL60: \nL61: ^{0}^{0}footnotetext: ^{⋄}Corresponding author.\nL62: ###### Abstract\nL63: This work introduces Hybrid Sparse Attention (HySparse), a new architecture that interleaves each full attention layer with several sparse attention layers. While conceptually simple, HySparse strategically derives each sparse layer’s token selection and KV caches directly from the preceding full attention layer. This architecture resolves two fundamental limitations of prior sparse attention methods.\nL64: First, conventional approaches typically rely on additional proxies to predict token importance, introducing extra complexity and potentially suboptimal performance. In contrast, HySparse uses the full attention layer as a precise oracle to identify important tokens. Second, existing sparse attention designs often reduce computation without saving KV cache. HySparse enables sparse attention layers to reuse the full attention KV cache, thereby reducing both computation and memory.\nL65: We evaluate HySparse on both 7B dense and 80B MoE models. Across all settings, HySparse consistently outperforms both full attention and hybrid SWA baselines. Notably, in the 80B MoE model with 49 total layers, only 5 layers employ full attention, yet HySparse achieves substantial performance gains while reducing KV cache storage by nearly $10\\times$.\nL66: ## 1 Introduction\nL67: \nL68: The demand for long-context capabilities has become a cornerstone of modern Large Language Models (LLMs), driven by emerging paradigms such as test-time scaling (cite34†Jaech et al., 2024 ; cite35†Guo et al., 2025 ) and agentic workflows (cite36†Kimi Team et al., 2025a ; cite37†Anthropic, 2024 ). Yet, the self-attention mechanism in standard Transformers scales quadratically with sequence length, causing computational latency and cost to grow prohibitively as context length increases.\nL69: Sparse attention offers a straightforward and effective solution to mitigate this quadratic bottleneck (cite38†Child et al., 2019 ). Sparse attention computes attention over a selected subset of important tokens rather than all tokens in the sequence. Existing methods can be broadly categorized into training-free and trainable approaches.\nL70: Training-free methods rely on fixed patterns or heuristic to select important tokens (cite39†Zhang et al., 2023 ; cite40†Xiao et al., 2023 ; cite41†Jiang et al., 2024 ; cite42†Tang et al., 2024 ; cite43†Yang et al., 2025b ; cite44†Xiao et al., 2024 ). Trainable sparse attention learns which tokens to attend during training, either via low-cost self-distillation (cite45†Gao et al., 2024 ; cite46†Liu et al., 2025a ) or by being directly integrated into pre-training (cite47†Yuan et al., 2025 ; cite48†Lu et al., 2025 ).\nL71: Nevertheless, sparse attention architectures still suffer from two fundamental limitations:\nL72: (1) Proxy-based Sparse Token Selection. Sparse attention fundamentally depends on a selection mechanism to identify important tokens prior to attention computation. Existing methods typically rely on lightweight proxies, such as predefined patterns, heuristics, approximate estimates, or additional selection modules (cite41†Jiang et al., 2024 ; cite45†Gao et al., 2024 ; cite48†Lu et al., 2025 ; cite49†Gao et al., 2025 ; cite47†Yuan et al., 2025 ; cite46†Liu et al., 2025a ).\nL73: However, these proxies are inherently approximate and may fail to capture true token importance, particularly in long and evolving contexts. As a result, sparse token selection is often bounded by the fidelity of the proxy, potentially limiting the expressive power of sparse attention.\nL74: While learnable sparse attention alleviates selection errors by learning token selection during training, it does not fundamentally eliminate the proxy-based bottleneck and introduces additional selection modules that increase training complexity.\nL75: (2) Computation Reduction without Memory Relief. Modern sparse attention methods increasingly adopt dynamic sparsity to preserve model fidelity. Unlike static patterns (e.g., fixed strides or block structures), which can reduce KV cache storage but often incur noticeable performance degradation, dynamic approaches typically retain the full KV cache. This is because complete KV cache eviction is irreversible and destructive, as token importance may shift as generation progresses and context evolves.\nL76: While dynamic sparse attention can effectively reduce computation, it provides no relief for memory consumption. Maintaining a full-sized KV cache therefore remains a dominant bottleneck for serving throughput and maximum batch size, limiting the practical benefits of sparse attention in long-context settings.\nL77: To address these challenges, we introduce Hybrid Sparse Attention (HySparse). The key idea is to interleave every full attention layer with multiple sparse attention layers, where the sparse layers strategically derive important token selection and KV caches from the preceding full layer.\nL78: This design is motivated by two empirical observations in recent literature: token saliency is stable across consecutive layers (§cite10†2.3 ), and cross-layer KV cache sharing reduces memory without hurting performance (§cite11†2.4 ). In HySparse, full attention can precisely identify important token selection and already produces KV caches, which sparse layers can directly reuse. By reusing the important token indices from full attention, sparse selection becomes oracle-guided.\nL79: This eliminates the need for auxiliary proxy modules and ensures stable end-to-end training. By reusing the KV caches from full attention, sparse attention adds no per-layer KV overhead, effectively alleviating the memory pressure associated with dynamic sparse attention.\nL80: Meanwhile, inspired by hybrid sliding window attention (SWA) architectures (cite50†Gemma Team et al., 2025 ; cite51†Agarwal et al., 2025 ; cite52†Xiaomi Team et al., 2026 ), HySparse augments each sparse attention layer with an additional SWA branch that maintains a small, local KV cache to enhance short-range modeling capacity.\nL81: We evaluate HySparse on both 7B dense and 80B Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) model settings. For the 7B dense model, we adopt a full-to-sparse layer ratio of 1:3, while for the 80B MoE model, a more aggressive 1:11 ratio is used. In both cases, the final layer employs full attention to preserve global aggregation. Across tasks and context lengths, HySparse consistently outperforms both full attention and hybrid SWA baselines, without incurring any additional KV cache cost relative to the hybrid SWA baseline.\nL82: Remarkably, in the HySparse 80B MoE model with 49 total layers, only 5 layers use full attention, meaning nearly $10\\times$ KV cache reduction, while the models still delivers substantial performance gains. Compared with Hybrid SWA, HySparse can significantly reduce the number of full attention layers, effectively pushing the hybrid ratio to its limit.\nL83: In summary, these results indicate that HySparse provides a simple and effective architectural solution to the core limitations of sparse attention, achieving strong long-context modeling capability with clear efficiency and memory advantages.\nL84: ## 2 Background & Motivation\nL85: ### 2.1 Training-free vs. Trainable Sparse Attention\nL86: Sparse attention methods can be divided into training-free and trainable approaches. Training-free methods rely on fixed patterns or heuristics to identify important tokens. They are applied as a drop-in modification at inference, enabling fast sparsity decisions with minimal computational cost (cite40†Xiao et al., 2023 ; cite42†Tang et al., 2024 ; cite44†Xiao et al., 2024 ; cite39†Zhang et al., 2023 ).\nL87: However, applying sparsity only at inference creates a training–inference mismatch, which may lead to error accumulation in long decoding or multi-step reasoning (cite53†Hu et al., 2026 ; cite54†Liu et al., 2025b ; cite55†He and Lab, 2025 ). Trainable sparse attention methods, in contrast, learn token importance during training through lightweight selection modules.\nL88: By integrating sparsity into the training process, they improve alignment between training and inference, selecting more informative tokens with higher recall and overall accuracy (cite45†Gao et al., 2024 ; cite49†Gao et al., 2025 ; cite46†Liu et al., 2025a ; cite47†Yuan et al., 2025 ; cite48†Lu et al., 2025 ; cite56†MiniCPM Team, 2025 ; cite57†Zhao et al., 2025 ). However, training these selection modules are non-trivial.\nL89: One approach uses auxiliary losses, such as self-distillation, to align the gating or indexer module with the original dense attention (cite45†Gao et al., 2024 ; cite49†Gao et al., 2025 ; cite46†Liu et al., 2025a ). These methods are simple but suboptimal. Alternatively, NSA (cite47†Yuan et al., 2025 ) performs end-to-end sparse pretraining by injecting the compressed attention (selection module) output into the main attention.\nL90: This design allows the selection module to receive learning signals only indirectly through the final attention output, without direct supervision on its token selection decisions.\nL91: ### 2.2 Hybrid Attention Architecture\nL92: To reduce quadratic compute and KV cache costs, hybrid attention has emerged as a promising solution for scaling context length. For instance, MiniMax-01 (cite58†Li et al., 2025a ) integrates both linear attention and softmax attention mechanisms in a structured pattern. Similarly, Qwen3-Next (cite59†Qwen Team, 2025 ) and Kimi Linear (cite60†Kimi Team et al., 2025b ) incorporate Gated DeltaNet (cite61†Yang et al., 2024b ) or its variants.\nL93: The Nemotron family (cite62†Basant et al., 2025 ; cite63†Blakeman et al., 2025 ) and Jamba (cite64†Lieber et al., 2024 ) integrate Mamba modules (cite65†Gu and Dao, 2023 ; cite66†Dao and Gu, 2024 ) with standard self-attention modules. Models such as GPT-OSS (cite51†Agarwal et al., 2025 ), Gemma3 (cite50†Gemma Team et al., 2025 ), and MiMo-V2-Flash (cite52†Xiaomi Team et al., 2026 ) employ a heterogeneous interleaving of sliding window attention and global full attention layers.\nL94: The sliding window size can be as small as 128 tokens with negligible KV cache overhead. Yet, the hybrid model with dynamic sparse attention has not been fully explored.\nL95: ### 2.3 Cross-Layer Salient Token Stability\nL96: Several concurrent works have observed that salient tokens (sparse tokens with higher attention scores) tend to remain relatively stable across consecutive layers in standard transformer models (cite67†Yang et al., 2024a ; cite68†Hao et al., 2025 ; cite69†Yang et al., 2025a ; cite70†Zarch et al., 2025 ; cite71†Deshmukh et al., 2025 ). These methods exploit this property to accelerate inference as a training-free manner.\nL97: Specifically, they identify important tokens using a full attention layer and reuse the salient token indices in subsequent layers to perform sparse attention computation. Inspired by these works, we elevate this empirical observation to a hybrid attention architecture for pretraining, in which full attention layers identify important tokens that are subsequently reused by following sparse attention layers.\nL98: ### 2.4 Cross-layer KV Cache Sharing\n"
